In France, voting traditionally involves a low-tech experience: voters manually place in the ballot box a paper ballot, pre-printed with the candidates' names, which they have selected themselves.
After officials have verified the voter's identity, the voter inserts the ballot into the voting box and signs the ballot.
French election law strictly codifies these procedures.
Since 1988, ballot boxes are required to be transparent to ensure that voters and observers can verify that no ballots were present at the beginning, that ballots were counted properly, and that no additional ballots were added during the voting process except those properly authorized.
Candidates may send representatives to observe all stages of the process. At night, votes are counted under strict supervision by scrutineers, in accordance with special regulations.
