When a small group of living organisms (a small population) becomes separated from the main population (for example, when they move across a mountain range or a river, or when they migrate to a new island and cannot easily return), they often find themselves in a distinctly different environment than the one they previously inhabited.
This new environment has different resources and different competitors, so they need additional features or adjustments to become a stronger competitor than previously demanded by the market.
The population remains absolutely unchanged; they are just as in need of adaptation as before.
As time passes, the population gradually becomes less and less similar to other populations as it adapts to its new environment.
Eventually, after thousands, and even millions of years, the two populations have become so different that they can no longer be considered members of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which refers merely to the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a very important aspect of evolution.
