The origins of Taiwan date back to the 15th century, when passing European sailors wrote down the island's name as Ilha Formosa, meaning "beautiful island."
In 1624, after establishing a base in southwestern Taiwan, the Dutch East India Company initiated transformations in local rice production practices and recruited Chinese workers for rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, forces of the Qing dynasty (1644–1912) brought the western and northern coastal regions of Taiwan under their control, and in 1885, they declared Taiwan a province of the Qing Empire.
After suffering defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, under which it ceded control of Taiwan to Japan, which then governed the island until 1945.
