Outside the savanna, it is difficult for primates, like humans, with digestive systems adapted to human-like nutrition, to meet their amino acid requirements from available plant sources.
In addition, being unable to do so leads to serious consequences: increased depression, malnutrition, and ultimately death.
Proteins found in yams and tuber crops would be among the most easily cultivated plant sources, but for primates like us, digesting them is difficult unless they are cooked.
On the other hand, organismic foods (maggots, termites, eggs) are not only easily digestible but also provide high levels of protein containing all essential amino acids.
All things considered, if our ancestors solved their "protein problems" in a manner similar to today's savanna chimpanzees, we shouldn't be surprised.
Sleep inertia is the process of waking up earlier than your usual sleep time and then going back to sleep shortly afterward (within 10–60 minutes).
You can easily achieve this by using a relatively quiet alarm clock that gently wakes you up without startling you.
If you're turning off the alarm while still asleep, you can place it in another corner of the room, which will require you to get up from your bed to turn it off.
Other biorhythm-based approaches involve drinking a large amount of liquid (especially water or tea, known as a diuretic) before sleep, which forces one to get up to urinate.
