Many rhythms observed in physiology and behavior are ultimately linked to the presence of endogenous cycles and their generation through biological clocks.
Periodic rhythms that are not merely simple responses to external rhythmic signals have been documented in many living organisms, including bacteria, fungi, plants, and animals.
Biological clocks are autonomous oscillators that continue their rhythmic activity even in the absence of external signals.
The idea that DNA is genetic material was one of the main hypotheses of the Hershey and Chase experiment.
Xershi and Cheyz used phages or viruses to transfer their DNA into bacteria.
They labeled the DNA in the phage with radioactive phosphorus and the phage's protein with radioactive sulfur, carrying out two experiments.
Mutations may have different effects depending on the type of mutation, the importance of the affected genetic material, and whether the affected cells are sex cells or not.
Only mutations in sex cells can be passed on to children, while mutations in any other cells may lead to cell death or cancer.
Nature-based tourism attracts people who are interested in visiting natural areas to enjoy the wilderness, including plants and animals.
Examples of activities at these locations include hunting, fishing, photography, birdwatching, visiting gardens, and learning about ecotourism.
For example, visiting Borneo to photograph and study orangutans.
Every morning, people drive past small towns to go to work, and they meet with people whose workplaces are in the very places they've just passed through.
In this dynamic transportation network, each person is somehow connected to a personal car-based transportation system and supports it.
Currently, science indicates that this large carbon economy has deprived the biosphere of the stable conditions it has maintained over the past 2 million years, which supported human evolution.
Each person is a participant in society and uses transportation systems. Nearly all people make use of transportation systems.
In developed countries, you will rarely witness complaints about the quality of drinking water or bridges collapsing, like these.
Why do transport systems generate such complaints, and why do they make errors every day? Are transport engineers simply incompetent? Or is there something even more significant at play?
Traffic flow refers to the study of individual drivers and vehicles' movements between two points, as well as their mutual interactions.
Unfortunately, studying traffic flow is challenging because drivers' behaviors cannot be predicted with 100% accuracy in advance.
In fact, drivers tend to maintain a consistent and rational spacing, which results in traffic flows that exhibit a degree of stability and can be approximately described mathematically.
To better represent traffic flow, the following relationships among three fundamental characteristics are established: (1) flow, (2) density, and (3) speed.
These circumstances assist in planning, designing, and operating road objects.
The first animals to take flight were insects. Their ability to fly helped them escape predators more easily and find food and mates more efficiently.
Most insects have the ability to flap their wings along their bodies.
This provides them with larger areas of smaller territories to escape predators.
Among insects today, only dragonflies and mayflies are unable to fold their wings.
Around several thousand years ago, a man named Aristarchus stated that the solar system revolves around the Sun.
Some people considered it true, but most were convinced of the opposite; that is, the solar system, including the Sun (and even other stars), revolves around the Earth.
That makes sense, because the Earth seems not to be moving, doesn't it?
The Amazon is the largest and second-longest river on Earth. It carries eight times more water than the second-largest river.
Also, the Amazon is the world's widest river, reaching up to six miles in width in some places.
The entire 20 percent of the water flowing from rivers into the oceans comes from the Amazon River.
The main Amazon River is 6,387 km (3,980 miles). It receives water from thousands of smaller rivers.
