Modern Casablanca was founded in the 10th century by Berber fishermen and later used as a strategic port by Phoenicians, Romans, and Mauritanians, known as Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed it and rebuilt it under the name Kasa Branka, after the earthquake of 1755.
In the 16th century, the Moroccan sultan rebuilt the city as Daru l-Badya and named it Kasablanka, after the Spanish merchants who established markets there.
Casablanca is one of the most interesting places in all of Morocco for shopping.
It's easy to find places around the old Medina selling traditional Moroccan products such as clay pots, ceramic dishes, charm items, carpets, and various ornaments, but all of them are intended for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, located in the far east near Rwanda.
In 2002, the Nyiragongo volcano lava flow destroyed the streets of Goma city, especially those in the city center, reducing them to ashes.
Even though Goma is sufficiently secure, any travel beyond Goma requires careful consideration due to the ongoing clashes constantly occurring in North Kivu province.
The city is also a base for climbing Nyiragongo Volcano, along with Africa's cheapest gorilla trekking.
You can also use moto-taxis (boda-boda) to tour Goma. For short trips, the regular (local) fare is about 500 Congolese francs.
Along with its limited accessibility, "Timbuktu" began to be used as a metaphor for distant, exotic lands.
Today, despite its reputation and the presence of an airport, Timbuktu remains a modest little town.
Due to the threat posed by desert sands, in 1990 it was included in the List of World Heritage in Danger.
This was one of the main stops in the PBS special series "The Wonders of Africa" by Henry Louis Gates.
This city differs sharply from other cities in the country, as it is more Arab-influenced than African in style.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa, bordered by Mozambique to the east, Zimbabwe to the north, and the Limpopo River to the south.
The reserve covers an area of 19,500 km² and is divided into 14 ecological zones, each supporting distinct wildlife.
It is one of the main attractions in Southern Africa and is regarded as the flagship of South Africa's National Parks (SANParks).
Scientists are working on creating a reactor capable of producing energy using this very method.
It might be beneficial to purchase a Wild Card that provides access to selected parks in Southern Africa or all national parks in Southern Africa.
Hong Kong Island, which gave its name to the Hong Kong region, is a major destination attracting numerous tourists.
The cluster of buildings forming Hong Kong's skyline resembles a distinct, shiny striped pattern due to the presence of Victoria Harbour's waters.
To see Hong Kong's best views, head out from the island and walk to the waterfront at Kowloon, across the bay.
A significant portion of Hong Kong's urban development is concentrated on reclaimed land along the northern coastline.
This was once considered the territory of the British colonizers, so if you're looking for evidence of the region's colonial past, this place is an excellent starting point.
The Sundarbans stretch 80 km (50 miles) from the coastal area into the interior of Bangladesh and India, making it the largest contiguous mangrove forest in the world.
The Sundarbans were declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The portion of the forest located in India is known as the "Sundarbans National Park."
Yet, forests are not just mangrove swamps — they also include the last remaining trees from the vast, ancient mangrove forests that once covered the Indian subcontinent.
The Sundarbans cover an area of approximately 3,850 km², with about one-third of the region being covered by water/wetland areas.
The Sundarbans Wildlife Sanctuary has existed since 1966, and it is estimated that the region currently hosts around 400 royal Bengal tigers and nearly 30,000 spotted deer.
