The Oyapok River Bridge is a suspension bridge supported by cables. It stretches along the Oyapok River, connecting the Brazilian city of Oyapok with the French Guianan town of Saint-Georges-de-l'Oyapok.
The two minarets reach a height of 83 meters and have two walkways, each 378 meters long and 3.5 meters wide.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Construction was completed in August 2011, but it remained closed to vehicle traffic until March 2017.
The bridge is scheduled to be fully operational in September 2017, when the construction of the Brazilian border control checkpoints is expected to be completed.
The Guarani are currently the most significant indigenous community residing in the eastern regions of Paraguay and have lived as semi-nomadic hunters engaged in subsistence agriculture.
In the Chaco region, other groups of indigenous communities, such as the Guaykurú and Payagua, have lived and sustained their livelihoods through hunting, fishing, and gathering.
Paraguay, which was formerly known as the "Great Province of the Indians," emerged in the 16th century as a result of conflicts between Spanish conquerors and local indigenous groups.
The Spanish began their colonial era, which lasted for three centuries.
Since its founding in 1537 as Asunción, Paraguay has succeeded in preserving much of its original characteristics and unique identity.
Argentina is one of the world's top polo teams and is famous for its players.
The world's largest polo tournament is held annually at the polo stadiums in Las Vegas during December.
In other times of the year, smaller competitions and matches can also be seen here.
Contact the Argentina de Polo Association for information on where to purchase polo game tickets and the latest news about tournaments.
The official currency of Folklend is the Folklend pound (FKP), which is pegged at par with the British pound (GBP).
Currency exchange is available at the only bank on the island opposite the FIC West store in Stenli.
British pounds are widely accepted throughout the islands, and both Sterling credit cards and US dollars are frequently accepted as well.
Although British and U.S. currencies may be accepted, credit cards might not be accepted in remote islands; be sure to check in advance with the owners about which payment methods are accepted.
It is almost impossible to exchange Folklend currency outside the islands, so please exchange your money before leaving the islands.
Because Montevideo is located south of the equator, when it is winter in the Northern Hemisphere, it is summer there, and vice versa.
Montevideo is located in a subtropical zone; it is common for temperatures to exceed +30°C during summer months.
Winter can feel chillingly cold: temperatures rarely drop below the freezing point, but with wind and humidity combined, it can feel significantly colder than the thermometer reading.
There are no distinct "rainy" and "dry" seasons: the amount of rainfall remains almost constant throughout the year.
Although most of the animals in the park are accustomed to seeing people, the wild nature of wildlife is unquestionably wild, and they must not be fed or disturbed.
According to the recreation park authorities, stay at least 100 yards/meters away from bears and wolves, and at least 25 yards/meters away from all other wild animals!
Large animals such as bison, elk, moose, and bears, despite their size, can still be vulnerable to surprise attacks.
Each year, dozens of visitors get injured because they fail to maintain a safe distance. Animals can be unpredictable, wild, and dangerous, so it's essential to respect their personal space.
Also, odors from food attract bears and other wild animals, so avoid carrying or cooking fragrant foods and keep your camp clean.
Apia is the capital of Samoa. The city is located on the island of Upolu and has fewer than 40,000 residents.
Apia was founded in the 1850s and has since 1959 been recognized as Samoa's official capital.
